import json
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).parent
answers={}
def add(sec,nums,body,ref=None):
    if isinstance(nums,int):nums=[nums]
    for n in nums:
        key=f'AAPP-{sec:02d}-{n:03d}'
        assert key not in answers,key
        answers[key]=body.strip()+(('\n\n**Reference:** '+ref) if ref else '')
base='https://www.mpi-forum.org/docs/mpi-4.1/mpi41-report/'
def mpiref(node,label):return f'[{label}]({base}node{node}.htm).'
standard='[MPI standard, version 4.1](https://www.mpi-forum.org/docs/mpi-4.1/mpi41-report/mpi41-report.htm).'
bcast='For an intracommunicator, `MPI_Bcast(buffer,count,datatype,root,comm)` copies the root\'s data to every member of `comm`, including the root itself. All members call it with the same root/communicator and compatible type signatures. `buffer` is the source at the root and destination elsewhere; `count` is the number of datatype entries, `datatype` describes their layout/type, and `root` is a rank in that communicator. Allocate valid buffers before calling. Blocking return makes the local buffer usable; it does not promise that all other processes have returned, so broadcast is not a general barrier.'
gather='For an intracommunicator, `MPI_Gather(sendbuf,sendcount,sendtype,recvbuf,recvcount,recvtype,root,comm)` collects one equally sized contribution from every rank, including root, and stores them at root in rank order. `sendcount` describes the local contribution; **`recvcount` is the count per rank, not the total**. For $p$ ranks sending $k$ ordinary elements each, root needs space for $pk$ elements. Receive-buffer arguments matter only at root; send and receive type signatures must match pairwise. Use `MPI_Gatherv` for variable contribution sizes. All ranks participate; completion is not a general barrier.'
reduce='`MPI_Reduce(sendbuf,recvbuf,count,datatype,op,root,comm)` combines corresponding entries from all processes and returns the result at root. With `count=1` it reduces one scalar per process; with `count=k` it produces **k reduced elements**, where output position $j$ combines position $j$ across ranks. It does not reduce all positions into one scalar. The operation must meet MPI\'s associativity rules and be valid for the datatype; examples are `MPI_SUM` and `MPI_MAX`. All members supply compatible arguments. Use `MPI_Allreduce` when every rank needs the result; floating-point reassociation can alter rounding.'
communicator='An MPI intracommunicator combines a group of processes, ranked locally, with an isolated communication context. The context prevents matching messages from unrelated communications even when ranks/tags coincide. Create communicators at runtime with operations such as `MPI_Comm_split` or duplicate one with `MPI_Comm_dup`. For example, in a 2D distributed matrix, create one communicator per process row and one per process column, then broadcast row/column blocks independently within those groups. Collective calls involve the specified group, not automatically every process in the application. Free custom communicators when finished.'
split='For a 2D matrix distributed over a grid of ranks, split the world communicator by process-row number, ordering members by column number. Each resulting row communicator broadcasts a matrix block only along that row, with local ranks and an independent context. Create column communicators similarly for column broadcasts. `MPI_Comm_split` collectively groups processes with the same color and orders each group by key (ties by original rank); `MPI_UNDEFINED` excludes a process. This avoids forcing unrelated rows to participate in every row operation.'
sync='MPI normally uses **explicit synchronization** through the communication and completion operations selected by the programmer. A blocking receive waits for its matching message; a blocking send returns when its send buffer is reusable, which need not mean the receiver has completed. `MPI_Barrier` is an explicit rendezvous. Nonblocking calls initiate work and require completion with `MPI_Wait`/`MPI_Test` or their variants. Collectives require matching participation/order, but a data-moving collective is not automatically a barrier. Separate address spaces remove ordinary shared-variable races, not communication-order deadlocks.'
memory='MPI describes cooperating **processes**, normally with separate address spaces and explicit communication. It supports distributed-memory clusters as well as shared-memory nodes; an implementation may use shared memory internally for local communication, and MPI also provides shared-memory window facilities. Pthreads and ordinary host OpenMP primarily use threads and directly shared memory within a node. MPI ranks are processes, not individually addressable threads, although each process can itself be multithreaded. Hybrid MPI+OpenMP uses message passing across nodes and threads within nodes.'
deadlock=r'''Yes: communication dependencies can form a cycle even without shared memory. For example, ranks 0 and 1 both execute a blocking receive from the other before either sends; neither matching send will ever be posted. Likewise, both doing a standard blocking `MPI_Send` before receive can deadlock when buffering is unavailable. Fix a pairwise exchange with `MPI_Sendrecv`, a correct complementary send/receive order, or post `MPI_Irecv` and `MPI_Isend` before waiting for both. Never rely on eager buffering observed for small test messages.'''
nonblockdead='Nonblocking initiation does not fix an incorrect dependency order. For example, both ranks do `MPI_Irecv(...from_other...,&r); MPI_Wait(&r,...); MPI_Isend(...to_other...,&s);`. Each wait still prevents the matching send from being posted, so they deadlock. Post all required matching receives/sends before waits, or use `MPI_Sendrecv`. Wrong peers/tags or mismatched collective order can also prevent completion regardless of whether calls are nonblocking.'
overlap='To overlap communication with independent computation, initiate `MPI_Irecv`/`MPI_Isend`, compute without touching active communication buffers, and periodically call `MPI_Test` or finish with `MPI_Wait`. Progress/overlap depends on the implementation and how MPI is driven; initiation alone does not promise background transfer throughout arbitrary computation. Alternatively use one communication thread while other OpenMP threads compute. Call `MPI_Init_thread`, check the provided level, and keep MPI calls within that level: `FUNNELED` permits only the initializing/main thread, `SERIALIZED` permits nonconcurrent calls by threads, and `MULTIPLE` permits concurrent calls.'
probe=r'''Use a probe when the next message's size or kind is not known. For example, workers send variable-length search results; the coordinator probes a ready result, allocates exactly enough storage, and receives the same message:

```c
MPI_Status st;
MPI_Probe(MPI_ANY_SOURCE, RESULT_TAG, comm, &st);
int n;
MPI_Get_count(&st, MPI_DOUBLE, &n);
double *buf = malloc((size_t)n * sizeof *buf);
MPI_Recv(buf, n, MPI_DOUBLE, st.MPI_SOURCE, st.MPI_TAG,
         comm, MPI_STATUS_IGNORE);
```

Assume valid homogeneous double messages, representable counts, successful allocation and no competing receive that can steal the probed message. For concurrent receivers use matched probing (`MPI_Mprobe`/`MPI_Mrecv`) or serialize access. A probe inspects, not consumes, a message. Sending a size header first is an alternative, so probe is required by this particular protocol, not by every possible implementation of variable-size messaging.'''
tags='In the C binding the tag is an integer message label. Matching uses the communicator/context, source rank and tag. Sends use a valid nonnegative tag (bounded by `MPI_TAG_UB`); receives/probes can use `MPI_ANY_TAG` to accept any tag and inspect `status.MPI_TAG`. Example: worker rank 3 sends array lengths with `LENGTH_TAG=10` and numeric payloads with `DATA_TAG=11`; the receiver requests the corresponding tag so a length is not confused with data. A dispatcher may probe any tag, then branch on WORK/RESULT/STOP and receive the identified source/tag.'
startup='`mpirun -np p program` (or the implementation/scheduler equivalent) launches and connects the requested MPI processes, possibly on multiple nodes, establishing the runtime environment in which MPI initialization forms the job. Running an executable directly may work as a singleton in some implementations, so `mpirun` is not universally the only valid launch method; scheduler commands such as `srun` can serve that role. Ordinary direct execution does not by itself request a distributed p-process run.'
implement='MPI is a **standard/API specification**; **Open MPI** and **MPICH** are independent implementations of it. An implementation provides headers/modules, communication/runtime libraries, compiler wrapper commands such as `mpicc`, launch/integration tools and supporting components. It can select network transports, local shared-memory paths and collective algorithms for the target machine. Several implementations exist because hardware integration, optimization and development choices differ. Conforming programs should rely on standard guarantees, not an implementation\'s incidental buffering or timing.'
add(23,[1,10],sync,standard)
add(23,[2,11,18],bcast,mpiref(120,'MPI broadcast'))
add(23,[3,12,15,31,40,54],communicator,mpiref(180,'MPI intracommunicators'))
add(23,[4,7,14,21,44,50],memory,standard)
add(23,[5,13,19,38],gather,mpiref(122,'MPI gather'))
add(23,[8,16,43,52],reduce,mpiref(130,'MPI reduce'))
add(23,6,'For variable contiguous blocks from a root, compute `sendcounts[r]` and `displs[r]`, communicate each rank\'s local count, allocate its buffer, then call `MPI_Scatterv`. Each rank receives its own count; displacements at root are in units of the send datatype\'s extent. For irregular noncontiguous layouts, use suitable derived datatypes or pack buffers, and point-to-point or all-to-all variants when the required distribution is not a root scatter. Keep pairwise type signatures compatible and avoid overlapping root reads where prohibited.',mpiref(124,'MPI scatter and scatterv'))
add(23,9,'Yes. In the world model, initialize MPI before ordinary communication and finish requests before finalization. Match point-to-point source/tag/communicator and type signatures; allocate sufficient buffers. All members of a collective must make compatible collective calls in the same required order. Do not access active nonblocking receive buffers or modify active send buffers until completion. With threads, request/check an appropriate MPI thread-support level and obey it. These restrictions concern the protocol, not a general ban on mixing blocking/nonblocking calls or MPI with OpenMP.',standard)
add(23,17,'`MPI_Send(buf,count,datatype,dest,tag,comm)` performs a standard-mode blocking send. The buffer contains `count` datatype entries, addressed to rank `dest` in `comm` with message label `tag`. It returns when the sender may safely reuse the buffer. Depending on buffering/message protocol it may wait for a matching receive, so return does not necessarily mean the receiver has completed, and the call is not guaranteed to complete independently of the receiver. Arrange matching receives without a cyclic wait.',mpiref(67,'MPI communication modes'))
add(23,[20,30,51,59,65],tags,mpiref(60,'MPI message envelope'))
add(23,[22,58],'Two common strategies are: **(1)** know the size from the protocol (a fixed size, a safe upper bound, or an initial size/header message), allocate that capacity, then receive; **(2)** use `MPI_Probe` plus `MPI_Get_count`, allocate for the reported number of datatype entries, then receive the probed source/tag. The receive count is capacity and can exceed the actual message length; use the returned status to inspect what arrived. For multithreaded competing receives, matched probing avoids the probe/receive race.',mpiref(79,'MPI probe'))
add(23,[23,26,36,41,47,53,60],implement,standard)
add(23,24,'Point-to-point operations communicate between a sender and a matching receiver, identifying peers/tags within a communicator. Collective operations specify group-wide behavior, such as one-to-all broadcast, all-to-one gather, or reduction, and all relevant members must call compatible operations in the required order. A collective may use optimized trees/topology-aware algorithms internally. Blocking completion concerns local buffer safety and is not, in general, a full group barrier. Collectives do not use application tags to match the operation.',standard)
add(23,[25,37,39],deadlock,mpiref(68,'MPI point-to-point semantics'))
add(23,[27,46,67],overlap,mpiref(71,'MPI nonblocking communication'))
add(23,28,'With `default(none)`, `num_steps` and `step` need explicit data-sharing attributes if they are ordinary surrounding variables. `x` is covered by `private`, `sum` by the reduction, and the associated loop index `i` is predetermined private. A corrected directive is:\n\n```c\n#pragma omp parallel for private(x) reduction(+:sum) \\\n    default(none) shared(num_steps, step)\n```\n\nThis assumes those variables are declared and initialized appropriately; constants with predetermined rules may differ. Also reconstruct the extracted body as normal valid C. The issue is OpenMP scoping, despite this entry being grouped under MPI.')
add(23,[29,45],probe,mpiref(79,'MPI probe'))
add(23,[32,57],startup,mpiref(280,'MPI process startup'))
add(23,[33,49,70],split,mpiref(188,'MPI communicator constructors'))
add(23,34,'A blocking point-to-point call returns only after its local completion condition: a receive has placed its matched message in the receive buffer; a standard send has made the send buffer reusable. A send may rely on a matching receive rather than buffering, so two send-first peers can deadlock, and blocking also prevents the calling thread from doing independent work while waiting. Use a safe communication order, `MPI_Sendrecv`, or post nonblocking operations before their completion waits. A blocking call does not automatically stop all processes.',mpiref(67,'MPI communication modes'))
add(23,35,'For an SPMD benchmark, call `MPI_Barrier(comm)` before starting a timed phase so no process enters that phase until every group member has reached the barrier. For completion time, measure local elapsed time and reduce with maximum as appropriate. The barrier synchronizes control flow; it neither exchanges the benchmark data nor guarantees synchronized clocks or automatically completes unrelated outstanding nonblocking requests.',mpiref(119,'MPI barrier'))
add(23,[42],nonblockdead,mpiref(75,'MPI nonblocking semantics'))
add(23,48,'`count` is the maximum number of entries of `datatype` that the receiving buffer can hold, not the number of bytes unless the datatype is `MPI_BYTE`. Allocate storage covering that datatype/count layout. The actual message may contain fewer entries; a larger incompatible message causes truncation/error. Determine a sufficient capacity from the protocol or probing, and use `MPI_Get_count` on the returned status to obtain the actual number when applicable.',mpiref(62,'MPI receive'))
add(23,55,nonblockdead+'\n\n'+overlap,mpiref(71,'MPI nonblocking communication'))
add(23,56,'Ordinary `MPI_Scatter` sends the same count from root to each rank, and each process receives one block; therefore these are scalar per-block counts. At root, `sendcount` is not the total number of elements. If $N$ is not divisible by $p$, use `MPI_Scatterv` with `counts[r]=N/p+(r<N%p)` and prefix-sum displacements, or scatter equal blocks and handle leftovers explicitly. With `Scatterv`, each rank passes its own scalar receive count matching its particular contribution.',mpiref(124,'MPI scatter'))
add(23,61,r'''For ordinary contiguous blocks of `k` elements of type `T`, root sends rank $r$ the slice beginning at `r*k`. Root copies its own slice locally, avoiding a blocking send to itself:

```text
if rank == root:
    copy sendbuf[root*k : (root+1)*k] into recvbuf
    for r = 0 .. p-1:
        if r != root:
            MPI_Send(sendbuf + r*k, k, T, r, SCATTER_TAG, comm)
else:
    MPI_Recv(recvbuf, k, T, root, SCATTER_TAG, comm, status)
```

All ranks agree on `k`, root and tag; allocate valid nonoverlapping buffers. This is a correct simple linear scatter for these types, though an MPI collective can use a faster tree algorithm. Derived datatype extents require corresponding address calculations.''',mpiref(124,'MPI scatter'))
add(23,62,'Four processes run `echo hello world`, so the usual output consists of **four lines `hello world`**. Their order is unspecified, and launcher/output forwarding can affect presentation. `mpirun` can launch a non-MPI executable; `echo` does not itself need to initialize MPI to print its arguments. Interpret the printed long dash as the intended command option `-np 4`.')
add(23,63,'If “inside MPI” means the **standard**, expect definitions of the API and semantics: message matching, communicators, datatypes, collective behavior, completion/progress, thread support and language bindings. For example, it specifies what `MPI_Send` completion means and how `MPI_Gather` orders contributions. If it means an implementation\'s source, expect transport protocols, buffering, shared-memory/network paths, collective algorithms and runtime integration. MPI does not prescribe one universal source implementation.',standard)
add(23,64,r'''Use the initializing/main thread for communication and other threads for independent computation. Request and check `MPI_THREAD_FUNNELED`; then `master` in this outer team keeps calls on the initializing thread:

```c
/* MPI_Init_thread(..., MPI_THREAD_FUNNELED, &provided); check provided */
#pragma omp parallel shared(request)
{
    #pragma omp master
    {
        MPI_Irecv_c(/* receive arguments */, &request);
        MPI_Wait(&request, MPI_STATUS_IGNORE);
    }
    if (omp_get_thread_num() != 0) {
        do_independent_work_for_this_thread();
    }
} /* completion before consumers use the receive buffer */
```

This is an outline: divide work without duplication and do not access the receive buffer during the outstanding operation. A blocking `MPI_Recv` in the communication thread can serve the same overlap purpose. The printed `MPI_Test` is also missing a semicolon. If communication may occur on another thread, request `SERIALIZED` or `MULTIPLE` as required; arbitrary `single` is not sufficient for FUNNELED.''',mpiref(281,'MPI and threads'))
add(23,66,'For an ordinary single-threaded MPI program with private process memory and only message passing, a normal thread mutex is not needed to protect local data from other ranks: they cannot directly access it. MPI deadlocks still need a correct communication protocol; a mutex does not solve mismatched messages. This answer changes if the program uses MPI shared-memory windows, other shared resources or actual threads: then an appropriate interprocess/thread synchronization mechanism may be needed.',standard)
add(23,68,'**No deadlock arises from this snippet**, assuming exactly these operations, valid initialization and no external interference. Rank 0 first receives rank 2\'s tag-2 message, for which rank 2 has already posted (or will post) its send. It then receives rank 1\'s tag-1 message. Rank 1 may wait while rank 0 handles rank 2, but there is no cycle: rank 2 does not wait for rank 1. Standard-send buffering is unnecessary for this order. Each receive stores 10 into `x`, so rank 0 ends with `x==10`.',mpiref(68,'MPI point-to-point semantics'))
add(23,69,'A stencil halo exchange can overlap communication and useful computation: post nonblocking receives/sends for neighbor boundaries, compute interior cells that do not depend on incoming halos, wait for completion, then compute boundary cells. Do not modify active send buffers or read active receive buffers prematurely. This can reduce exposed communication latency when enough independent interior work exists and the implementation makes progress.',mpiref(71,'MPI nonblocking communication'))

cuda='https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/'
memref=f'[NVIDIA CUDA kernel/memory model]({cuda}02-basics/writing-cuda-kernels.html).'
mem='CUDA memory spaces include **registers** private to a thread; **local memory**, logically per-thread but normally backed by device memory when registers spill; **shared memory**, cooperatively used by a block; and **global device memory**, visible across kernels/threads with the required synchronization. Constant and texture/read-only access paths provide cached read-oriented spaces. Hardware caches (L1/L2) support these accesses but differ from programmer-managed shared memory. Host memory is another domain; explicit transfers or supported unified/system-memory mechanisms make data available. Scope, lifetime and access cost differ, so local memory is not automatically fast merely because it is thread-private.'
issues='CUDA requires enough independent work and a suitable kernel decomposition. Manage host/device data placement and transfers, use coalesced accesses, control register/shared-memory usage and occupancy, and avoid excessive branch divergence within warps. Synchronize only with valid scope: an ordinary block barrier does not synchronize all blocks. Small kernels, launch overhead and transfer cost can erase acceleration. Compared with a simple host OpenMP loop, CUDA often requires more explicit device-specific indexing, memory/lifetime management and tuning, although OpenMP offload also has data-transfer and device concerns. CUDA portability is tied to its supported NVIDIA platform.'
add(24,[1,7],mem,memref)
add(24,[2,6],issues,memref)
add(24,3,'**True** for ordinary operations enqueued in one stream: they execute in issue order, giving the required ordering of earlier operations before later dependent ones. Host submission can be asynchronous; operations in different streams may overlap when dependencies and hardware allow, but concurrency is not guaranteed. Default-stream synchronization behavior depends on its mode, so do not infer complete independence just from using different stream handles.',f'[NVIDIA CUDA asynchronous execution]({cuda}02-basics/asynchronous-execution.html).')
add(24,4,'CUDA is NVIDIA\'s parallel-computing platform and programming model. A host program launches kernels executed by many GPU threads, organized into blocks and grids, and coordinates memory movement and synchronization. CUDA provides programming-language support, runtime/driver APIs and libraries for general-purpose GPU computation. It is particularly effective for sufficiently large computations with substantial data parallelism and suitable memory behavior.',f'[NVIDIA CUDA programming model]({cuda}01-introduction/programming-model.html).')
add(24,5,'A kernel identifies its position using `threadIdx` within a block, `blockIdx` within a grid, and the dimensions `blockDim` and `gridDim`. For a 1D launch, the global element index is `i=blockIdx.x*blockDim.x+threadIdx.x`; guard with `if (i<n)` before accessing element `i`. Blocks and grids may have multiple dimensions; flatten them using the corresponding dimensions as needed. These are logical identifiers, not fixed physical core numbers.',memref)
add(24,8,'Threads are grouped into **blocks**, and blocks form a **grid** for a kernel launch. Threads in one block can cooperate using block-scoped shared memory and barriers. Hardware executes threads in **warps** (32 threads on NVIDIA GPUs), with divergence affecting execution efficiency. Blocks are scheduled onto streaming multiprocessors; ordinary blocks must not depend on a particular inter-block execution order, since some may execute later. A grid does not imply that all its threads are simultaneously resident.',memref)

add(25,1,'Halide is a domain-specific language embedded in C++ for image processing and related array pipelines. It separates the **algorithm**, which defines the values of functions over coordinates, from the **schedule**, which describes how those values are computed and stored. The same algorithm can therefore be tiled, fused, vectorized, threaded or mapped to a GPU without rewriting its mathematical definition. The compiler produces optimized code for the selected target.','[Halide scheduling tutorial](https://halide-lang.org/docs/tutorial/lesson_05_scheduling_1.html).')
add(25,2,'A Halide schedule controls evaluation and storage: loop order, splitting/tiling, vectorization, unrolling, parallel execution and where intermediate stages are computed/stored (`compute_at`, `compute_root`, etc.). It chooses how to realize the algorithm efficiently on a target while respecting its dependencies. Different schedules trade recomputation against memory traffic and locality; scheduling is separate from defining the function\'s values.','[Halide multi-stage scheduling](https://halide-lang.org/docs/tutorial/lesson_08_scheduling_2.html).')
add(25,3,'`split(x,x_outer,x_inner,4)` replaces the x traversal by an outer loop over groups of four and an inner loop over positions in a group, conceptually `x=x_min+4*x_outer+x_inner`. `vectorize(x_inner)` evaluates that inner dimension with SIMD lanes, nominally four x positions at once. The compiler handles any non-multiple-of-four tail according to the applicable tail strategy; this does not by itself create CPU threads.','[Halide split and vectorize](https://halide-lang.org/docs/tutorial/lesson_05_scheduling_1.html).')
add(25,4,'**True as a useful scheduling heuristic**, not a required execution order or a guarantee of optimality. Start from the output/consumer loops, then decide where to compute and store the producer stages relative to those loops. The consumer\'s tiling and traversal determine producer regions and reuse, so this order makes locality/recomputation choices clearer. Runtime evaluation still respects producer-to-consumer dependencies, and schedules may be revised iteratively.','[Halide producer/consumer scheduling](https://halide-lang.org/docs/tutorial/lesson_08_scheduling_2.html).')
add(25,5,'**False.** High-level synthesis takes a higher-level behavioral/algorithmic description, commonly a supported subset of C/C++, together with constraints/directives, and produces a hardware implementation such as RTL. RTL instead describes registers, combinational logic and clocked transfers; it is a typical output of HLS and input to subsequent logic synthesis. Thus the statement reverses the relevant abstraction levels.','[AMD Vitis HLS introduction](https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/ug1399-vitis-hls/Introduction).')

qs=json.loads((root/'questions.json').read_text())
expected={q['id'] for q in qs if 23<=q['section']<=25}
assert set(answers)==expected,(sorted(expected-set(answers)),sorted(set(answers)-expected))
(root/'answers-mpi-cuda.json').write_text(json.dumps(answers,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(f'Prepared {len(answers)} model answers covering sections 23–25.')
